  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nTuesday night through next Monday:\n\nAnother day of hot/humid conditions as we remain on the edge of the \nhigh pressure sitting out near the Desert Southwest. A frontal \nboundary will also begin to slide down, providing a slight relief \nfrom humidity in northeast counties, however temps will remain in \nthe mid/upper 90s to near 100 degrees with areawide heat indices \nabove 105 F, with some areas reaching indices in the 110-115F range. \n\nA heat advisory and extreme heat warning will be in effect again \nWednesday given there isn't much change in the current scenario. \nWith daytime heating and an approaching boundary, showers/storms \nwill be possible Wednesday afternoon, and this could bring an \nearlier end to the heat over ne counties, but it will still be \nplenty hot before that happens, and so will keep this area under the \nheat advisory for now. One thing worth mentioning for Wednesday -\nnothing is outlooked for our CWA at this time, however, a Marginal \n(1 out of 5) risk for severe weather is outlooked near Mid AL, and \nthis may expand into our area due to decent instability in place. \nThe boundary is expected to make it farther south into our area and \nstall Thursday, but there is uncertainty in how far south the front \nmakes it. \n\nAs the high pressure retreats and keeps northwest flow over the \narea, another frontal boundary will push into the area from the late \nFriday into Saturday. This will keep rain chances through the \nweekend and into next week as this front stalls over the area as \nwell. /SW/",
